{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>If it bites.</b> It reverts to the matter above, namely \"and one who breaks through a fence, a snake bites him.\" It is possible that this fence is the fence that the ruler made, and he likened the slanderer to a snake. The meaning: the master of the tongue has no advantage over the venomous serpent — if it does not hear an incantation it harms, and does not benefit [him]."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "The Hebrew of the last clause is compressed; read literally as 'that does not hear an incantation, it harms and does not benefit.'"}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:28:59.363448669Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}